        "thinking": "I'll stick to my own sandbox for reference and start designing the capybara with realistic proportions—roughly 1.2m long, 0.5m shoulder height, 50kg, with a barrel-shaped body, blunt box-like head, small ears, and eyes and nostrils positioned high for its semi-aquatic lifestyle.\n\nFor the whimsical touches, I'm adding a tiny bright blue songbird perched on its head and considering a small flower tucked behind the ear for charm, while keeping the composition simple rather than adding extra props like a yuzu fruit that would widen the scene unnecessarily. I'll go with soft, painterly colors and mottled fur with subtle countershading rather than a flat toon style.\n\nNow I'm planning the actual proportions and skeleton: a chunky barrel-shaped body about 1.1m long with short stubby legs and an oversized blocky head to push the whimsical exaggeration, landing around 0.55m tall at the head and 0.67m with the bird included. I'm laying out the rig with a hips root, a spine chain running from rump to chest, a short neck into a big head with a separate jaw, then building the body shape by lofting a profile along spine path points from the rump forward to the chest.\n\nThe head gets its own blocky loft, going from the back of the skull forward to a blunt, bulbous Roman-nose muzzle with nostrils positioned high and forward, giving the capybara that flat-sloping forehead and tall blunt snout look, all centered higher up at roughly y 0.44.\n\nFor the skeleton I'm planning a hips root joint at the rump, then a spine chain running forward toward the chest, followed by a short two-joint neck chain leading into the head joint -- building this as a connected chain structure so each segment inherits from its parent properly.\n\nPlacing the head joint pointing forward and slightly down, then working out local coordinate conventions for attaching head geometry via loft stations along the forward axis.\n\nI'm thinking the head/snout should be one continuous loft shap