underneath — checking that the lowest points sit right near the floor. Then I move on to head details, setting up local head coordinates using its forward/up/left orientation.\n\nWorking out the loft stations along the head's forward axis, placing ellipse cross-sections at increasing forward offsets to shape the skull, cheeks, and tapering muzzle toward a blunt rounded nose end.\n\nChecking proportions against body scale -- head width 0.30 vs body 0.53 feels right for a whimsical blunt-headed capybara -- and adjusting vertical placement so the head sits properly above the neck and chest joints. Adding a small nose bulb sphere near the snout tip for the dark nose pad detail.\n\nI'm positioning the nostril ellipsoids and pink nose pad on the upper muzzle front, then setting up the lower jaw as a boxy capsule shape hinged at the jaw joint, with the upper lip hanging slightly over it for that droopy capybara mouth look.\n\nSetting the jaw joint position and adding a closed mouth with a thin dark seam line that curves upward slightly at the corners for a whimsical smile, plus small cream teeth boxes beneath the upper lip. Now placing the eyes symmetrically on the sides of the head.\n\nI'm thinking of using a hemisphere with its axis pointing up, positioned at the eye center with a slightly larger radius, so it covers the upper half of the eyeball and gives a sleepy-eyed look from the sides and partially from the front.\n\nNext I'll add tiny white sparkle spheres for highlights, then move on to ears: small rounded oval shapes positioned near the top-back of the head, pointing up and outward.\n\nSince the plate is symmetric, the normal direction works out fine either way; I just need the ear as a thin beveled plate, and rather than figuring out exact outer-face orientation, I'll make the inner pink part a slightly smaller, slightly thicker extrude so it shows through on both sides, with brown for the outer rim.\n\nNow I'm placing the inner pink patch via a paint function over the extrude coordinates, marking points inside a small ellipse near the ear base as pink and the rest brown, which handles both faces automatically. For positioning the bird, I need to find the top of the head surface and raycast downward to locate where it perches.\n\nI'm designing the bird itself: a chubby 9-10cm songbird facing forward like the capybara, with a scaled sphere body (cream breast, blue back, darker cap, coral cheeks) using a countershading paint, a smaller head sphere, and a small beak.\n\nI'm adding the finer bird details -- cone-shaped beak with a jaw joint, black eyes with highlights, folded tapered wings with feather stripes, three fanning tail feathers, a small crest of tufts, and thin orange legs ending in toe fans gripping into the capybara's fur, all wired up through a joint hierarchy (body, head, jaw, wings, tail, legs).\n\nNow I'm working out the bird's world position by casting a ray down onto the head surface to find where it perches, then offsetting upward slightly without adjusting its pitch. I'm also planning cheek fur tufts as tapered blade-shaped cards colored from the coat paint to mimic the capybara's bristly fur texture.\n\nI'm designing the coat color palette — warm cocoa-caramel tones with a darker mottled back, lighter belly, and slightly redder-brown head with a whitish chin — and sketching a countershading function that blends a mottled dark/light pattern into a softer belly tone.\n\nI'm layering in a subtle grain texture for fur streaks, nesting it inside the mottle-countershade composition, then estimating paint texel density to keep roughly 2mm resolution across the body. For the face, I'm planning a few short dark whisker sweeps near the muzzle plus small bristles above.\n\nNow I'm adding a small dark stub for a tail nub at the rump joint, and sketching a daisy flower near the ear base — six white petal shapes ringed around a yellow center, positioned using a ring helper anchored to the head surface.\n\nPlacing it near the upper-left of the head, roughly between the ear and the eye but angled slightly forward so it doesn't clutter the ear itself. I'm also planning a grass blade sticking out of the mouth corner, sweeping it out and curving down and forward from the lips.\n\nFor the body details, I'm thinking about scattering tuft textures across the back using an SVG tuft shape colored with a tint, since the figure should stay colored rather than greyscale.\n\nFor the fur silhouette, leaning the cards backward creates a bristly edge, and I'm estimating around 350-500 scattered cards at small sizes, each built from a few triangles with a bend of about 25 degrees, giving roughly 4000 triangles total for the fur coverage.\n\nI'm adding small whimsical touches too: dark hoof-like claws, and blush marks on the head made from soft pink decals with a few short diagonal strokes for an anime-style look, positioned toward the front side of the face. Now I'm ready to start writing the actual code, beginning with a helper function for positioning.\n\nNow I need to check the loft signature and its options for bone, color, sides, caps, and section, along with the joint typing for direction and ring, before writing and typechecking the code.\n\n",
